// S8 (#1103): the promotion dataflow leaves a slot in its stack cell at three
// sites, and this probe forces the `collapsed` one. The second loop's inner
// header (leader 43) creates a phi for `i`, a later merge collapses it onto one
// value (the trivial-phi rule), and a still later merge contradicts the
// collapse, so that header carries no register value for `i`.
//
// `i` is reused across two loops: the first raises it to 3, the second
// decrements it. With the give-up, `i`'s cell is read inside the second loop
// only by its two decrements (`i = i - 1` on its own line, leader 75, and the
// same statement inside `if (pass > 1)`, leader 102), and the second loop's
// header phi takes exactly those two results as its back-edge values. The pass
// after the value flow writes the cell on the incoming edges that carry `i`.
// Without the give-up both decrements take the collapsed value (3) instead, so
// every iteration sets `i` back to 2 and `while (i >= 0)` never ends: removing
// the site from qbe_promotion.cpp makes this probe time out instead of
// printing -1/4.
fun f() {
  var i = 0;
  while (i < 3) { i = i + 1; }
  var pass = 0;
  while (i >= 0) {
    var j = 0;
    while (j < 2) { j = j + 1; }
    if (pass > 1) { i = i - 1; pass = pass + 1; continue; }
    i = i - 1;
    pass = pass + 1;
  }
  return str(i) + "/" + str(pass);
}

print f();
